{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>When you go out to war against your enemy.</b> We need to be informed of the shift of language from singular to plural several times, for it says: ״וְרָאִיתָ סוּס וָרֶכֶב״ — and afterward it says: ״עַם רַב מִמְּךָ״. And the commentators said that in the eyes of HaShem, blessed be He, they are all like a single horse and chariot, and if it says עַם רַב — that is, they are numerous compared to you, but not in the eyes of HaShem, blessed be He. And furthermore, ״כִּי תֵצֵא״ is in the singular, and afterward it says: ״וְהָיָה כְּקָרָבְכֶם״ in the plural. And the כַּ״ף of simile in ״כְּקָרָבְכֶם״ requires explanation, for it should have said בְּקָרְבְכֶם." "And I say that when there is peace among the enemies, then the war is more dangerous, for all of them are founded of one accord against HaShem and against His anointed; but when their heart is divided and they are not in a single alliance, the danger is not so great. And this is the miracle that the Holy One, blessed be He, performs for Israel: that even though at the beginning of their going out to war all the nations joined together as though they were all a single horse and chariot, like one man — nevertheless, כְּקָרָבְכֶם אֶל הַמִּלְחָמָה, in the very midst, HaShem will send confusion among them and they will scatter, each man from his brother, and each man's sword will be against his fellow. As it is written (Deuteronomy 28:7): ״בְּדֶרֶךְ אֶחָד יֵצְאוּ אֵלֶיךָ וּבְשִׁבְעָה דְרָכִים יָנוּסוּ לְפָנֶיךָ״ — for at the outset they are of one accord, but בֵּינֵי בֵּינֵי HaShem will send confusion among them to divide their hearts until all seven nations, each one of them, flees on a separate road by itself; as it is written (Psalms 21:13): ״כִּי תְשִׁיתֵמוֹ שֶׁכֶם״ etc. — that is, to divide them into fragments." "And this is what it says: ״כִּי תֵצֵא לַמִּלְחָמָה עַל אוֹיְבֶךָ״ — with no יוּ״ד next to the בֵּי״ת, indicating a single enemy. ״וְרָאִיתָ סוּס וָרֶכֶב עַם רַב מִמְּךָ״ — that is, a people so numerous all gathered together to the Valley of Shaveh at the outset like one man and like a single horse and chariot, and each nation says to its fellow: my horse is like your horse — and by this, faintheartedness will surely enter your heart. Therefore it says: do not fear, neither of them nor of their multitude, for in the end they will scatter, each man from his brother, as it is written: ״אַתֶּם קְרֵבִים הַיּוֹם לַמִּלְחָמָה עַל אוֹיְבֵיכֶם״ — in the plural, for before Israel even drew near to them, HaShem had scattered them and poured a spirit of distortion in their midst (Isaiah 19:14) and divided their hearts one from another. And correspondingly, the Holy One, blessed be He, performs a second miracle for Israel as well: just as at the time of going out to war they go out as few in number, as though no more than a single man of Israel had gone out against them, so too at the outset Israel appear few in the eyes of the enemies — but בֵּינֵי בֵּינֵי HaShem sends His help, so that the enemies see the shadow of the mountains as though they were men; in the manner spoken of (II Kings 7:6): ״וַה׳ הִשְׁמִיעַ אֶת מַחֲנֵה אֲרָם״ etc. ״קוֹל חַיִל גָּדוֹל״ etc. — from here is a proof that even when Israel are few, the Holy One, blessed be He, shows them to the nations as though they were many, in order to strike them with terror. Therefore it says: ״וְהָיָה כְּקָרָבְכֶם אֶל הַמִּלְחָמָה״, and the כַּ״ף of simile stands on the plural language in the word ״כְּקָרָבְכֶם״, to say that you appear as though you are many even though in truth it is not so, for you are the few. And this is a precious interpretation."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {0 "The phrase צְרִיכָה בֵּאוּר appears to be a parenthetical gloss that has entered the main text; translated as part of the sentence as found.", 1 "The phrase בֵּינֵי בֵּינֵי (literally 'between between') is an idiom for 'in the thick of it / when they draw near'; rendered by sense as 'in the very midst.' The word שֶׁכֶם in the Psalm citation can mean 'shoulder' or 'portion,' and the derash reads it as related to division.", 2 "The verse fragment ״אַתֶּם קְרֵבִים הַיּוֹם לַמִּלְחָמָה עַל אוֹיְבֵיכֶם״ is cited without an explicit address; it paraphrases Deuteronomy 20:3–4. The phrase בֵּינֵי בֵּינֵי is rendered as in comment 1."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:13:10.483658463Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}